---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const nested = `# defaults
[server]
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 3000
tags = ["stable"]

# project
[server]
port = 8080
tags = ["local"]

# result
server.host = "127.0.0.1"
server.port = 8080
server.tags = ["local"]`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Layers and resolution — dotcfg"
  description="Understand ConfigStack precedence, merging, missing sources, and environment overrides."
>
  <article class="[&_p]:max-w-[720px]">
    <span class="text-[13px] font-bold text-primary">ConfigStack</span>
    <h1 class="m-0 mb-5 mt-3 text-[clamp(38px,7vw,64px)] leading-[1.02] tracking-[-0.045em] max-[760px]:text-[44px]">
      Layers & resolution
    </h1>
    <p class="max-w-[680px] text-xl text-muted-foreground max-[760px]:text-lg">
      When you read from a ConfigStack, dotcfg combines its sources and works out the value your
      application should see. This page explains those rules before you have to depend on them.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Later layers win
    </h2>
    <p>
      The first layer has the lowest priority. Each layer after it may replace values from the
      layers before it. This ordering is called <strong>precedence</strong>: it answers “which value
      wins when more than one source provides the same setting?”
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Nested settings are combined
    </h2>
    <p>
      Replacing one nested setting should not make unrelated settings disappear. When both sides are
      maps or tables, ConfigStack combines their keys and only replaces the parts that overlap.
      This is often called a <strong>deep merge</strong>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="Example" code={nested} />
    <p>
      Notice that <code>server.host</code> survives because the project layer did not replace it.
      The port changes because both layers define it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lists behave differently. ConfigStack does not try to guess how two lists should be combined,
      so a later list replaces the earlier list. Other single values, such as strings, numbers, and
      booleans, replace earlier values too.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Sources can use different formats
    </h2>
    <p>
      One source can use TOML while another uses JSON or YAML. Each <code>DotCfg</code> reads its own
      file format first; ConfigStack then combines the resulting values. You do not need to convert
      all of your source files to the same format just to layer them.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      What happens when a source has no values?
    </h2>
    <p>
      A source file may not exist yet. That is fine: ConfigStack skips the missing source and keeps
      resolving the others. A file that exists but is valid and empty contributes no settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      A broken file is different. If a source contains malformed TOML, JSON, or YAML, ConfigStack
      returns the parse error instead of pretending that source was missing. This helps you notice a
      configuration mistake instead of silently running with older values.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Put environment variables on top
    </h2>
    <p>
      You can give the stack an environment prefix with <code>with_env_prefix()</code>. Matching
      environment variables then have higher priority than every file layer.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Rust"
      code={'let cfg = ConfigStack::new()\n    .layer("defaults", defaults)\n    .layer("project", project)\n    .with_env_prefix("APP")\n    .build()?;'}
    />
    <p>
      ConfigStack uses two underscores to mark a nested key. For example,{' '}
      <code>APP_DATABASE__PORT</code> overrides <code>database.port</code>. A normal underscore is
      kept as part of the key, so <code>APP_API_KEY</code> means <code>api_key</code>, not{' '}
      <code>api.key</code>.
    </p>
    <Callout type="info" title="Why two underscores?">
      <p>
        ConfigStack can discover settings that exist only in the environment. Using <code>__</code>
        for nesting lets it tell the difference between a nested path and a key whose real name
        contains an underscore.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p>
      The final order is therefore: earlier file layers, later file layers, then environment
      variables.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-[34px]">
      Next, learn <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/config-stack/reading-writing">how to read the
      result and choose which source receives writes</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
